# Starship Flight 15, worked in quantities: the launch of Booster 22 and
# Ship 42, the methane and oxygen they burn, and the heat of the ship's
# return after about 14 orbits. Units are SI in the cells and carry through
# every formula: a product or quotient combines them, a sum converts them,
# and a result with no dimension is a plain number.
#
# Every figure that comes from a published description of the flight is on
# the Flight15 sheet with a link to where it was read. SpaceX's own pages
# (spacex.com/launches) did not give their figures to a plain fetch, so the
# links are to the pages that repeat them: check them against SpaceX before
# relying on one. A figure that is NOT on Flight15 is an assumption, and is
# marked `assumed` in the Source column beside it.

# Standard gravity.
const g0 = 9.80665m.s^-2

# Stefan-Boltzmann constant.
const Sigma = 5.670374e-8W.m^-2.K^-4

# Earth's gravitational parameter, and its mean radius.
const GM = 3.986004418e14m^3.s^-2
const EarthRadius = 6371km

# Molar mass of carbon dioxide.
const MolarCO2 = 44.01g.mol^-1

# The figures read from the flight's published description.
const StackHeight = Flight15[Value; Item == 'StackHeight'].first()
const ShipHeight = Flight15[Value; Item == 'ShipHeight'].first()
const LaunchMass = Flight15[Value; Item == 'LaunchMass'].first()
const LiftoffThrust = Flight15[Value; Item == 'LiftoffThrust'].first()
const OrbitAltitude = Flight15[Value; Item == 'OrbitAltitude'].first()
const EntryTime = Flight15[Value; Item == 'AtmosphericEntry'].first()
const LandingBurnTime = Flight15[Value; Item == 'ShipLandingBurn'].first()

# The number of Raptor 3 engines on the booster and on the ship, and the
# Starlink V3 satellites carried (Flight15 sheet, Engines and Payload rows).
const BoosterEngines = 33
const ShipEngines = 6
const Satellites = 26

# Assumed: the mass of one Starlink V3 satellite, the ship's empty mass and
# the booster's empty mass with its landing reserve. None is in the sources.
const SatelliteMass = 2Mg
const ShipDryMass = 120Mg
const BoosterDryMass = 275Mg

# Assumed: the heat transfer coefficient of the belly-first ship (tuned so
# that the tiles end near their 1,400 °C rating), their emissivity, the
# effective specific heat of air hot enough to dissociate, and the heat of
# ablation and density of an ablative shield, for comparison with the tiles.
const HeatCoeff = 0.003
const Emissivity = 0.85
const HotAirCp = 5kJ.kg^-1.K^-1
const HeatOfAblation = 25MJ.kg^-1
const AblatorDensity = 270kg.m^-3

# The delta-v a rocket gets from its exhaust speed and mass ratio
# (Tsiolkovsky): the exhaust speed times the natural log of full over empty.
fn DeltaV(ve, start, end) = ve * ln(start / end)

# The kinetic energy of a mass moving at a speed.
fn Kinetic(m, v) = 0.5 * m * v ** 2

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is read from the published description of Flight 15 (13 November 2026,
# Starbase pad 2). Times are seconds after liftoff.

sheet Flight15

| Item             | Value    | What                                      | Source |
| ---------------- | -------- | ----------------------------------------- | ------ |
| StackHeight      | 124.4m   | Booster 22 and Ship 42 stacked            | [Space Launch Schedule](https://www.spacelaunchschedule.com/launch/starship-starlink-group-31-2-starship-flight-15), [Next Spaceflight](https://nextspaceflight.com/launches/details/8403/) |
| ShipHeight       | 52.1m    | Ship 42                                   | [Space Launch Schedule](https://www.spacelaunchschedule.com/launch/starship-starlink-group-31-2-starship-flight-15) (171 ft in [Tech Insider](https://tech-insider.org/spacex-starship-flight-15-november-dual-catch-2026/)) |
